-- title:
--   Coefficientwise extension of automorphisms of L·ℚ(X₁(N)) to ℚ̄
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero equipped with an algebra structure over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, so that there is a structural map $\operatorname{algebraMap} L\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$, i.e. of the Laurent series field over $L$, and assume $K =$ `laurentBaseChange L (x1FunctionField N)`, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the image of the $q$-expansion function field $\mathbb{Q}(X_1(N)) =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (Gamma1 N)` $\subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ under the coefficientwise ring map induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$. Let $\sigma$ be an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\bar\sigma$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar N`, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of $\mathbb{Q}(X_1(N))$, such that for every $f$ in that field and every $b \in K$: if the Laurent series underlying $f$ equals the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series underlying $b$, then the series underlying $\bar\sigma(f)$ equals the coefficientwise image of the series underlying $\sigma(b)$. Only existence of $\bar\sigma$ is asserted, not uniqueness.
--
--   This is the passage from a field of definition $L$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for automorphisms of the function field of $X_1(N)$, realised concretely inside Laurent series in $q$: an $L$-rational automorphism of the constant field extension $L\cdot\mathbb{Q}(X_1(N))$ is extended to a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-rational automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot\mathbb{Q}(X_1(N))$ compatible with the coefficientwise embedding. It is used, with $\sigma$ a level-$p$ involution, in the study of the special fibre and Hecke operators on models of $X_1(N)$.
